  <h2 id="sizing-vs-grading">Sizing and grading are related—but they are not the same</h2>
  <p><strong>Sizing</strong> defines who the garment is intended to fit and how the final customer reads the size offer. A customer-facing chart may use body measurements such as bust, waist and hip. <strong>Grading</strong> is the pattern-development work that creates the additional garment sizes from the approved base pattern.</p>
  <p>For activewear, the relationship matters because stretch fabrics do not behave like rigid woven clothing. The pattern, fabric recovery, support construction and intended ease all shape the finished garment. A simple proportional scale-up can change a high-rise legging or a supportive bra more than the buyer expects.</p>

  <h2 id="base-size">Start with an approved base-size sample</h2>
  <p>The first sample should do more than look good on a mannequin. It should confirm the intended fit in realistic movement. For leggings, review bend, squat, lunge and waist position. For sports bras, review the underband, strap comfort, coverage and activity level. For jackets and tops, check shoulder movement, sleeve length and how the style layers over the intended base product.</p>
  <div class="article-callout"><strong>Fit principle:</strong> grading can scale an approved pattern; it cannot correct the wrong base fit. Finalize the base-size garment and fabric behavior before asking the factory to grade the range.</div>

