  <h2 id="size-chart">What belongs in a production-ready activewear size chart?</h2>
  <p>A useful size chart is not one line that says “S/M/L/XL.” It creates a shared reference for the brand, pattern team, sample room and inspection team. The exact format can vary, but the production document should make the following points clear.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Style identification:</strong> style name or code, product category and revision date.</li>
    <li><strong>Size range and base size:</strong> state which sample size was approved before grading.</li>
    <li><strong>Garment points of measure:</strong> use named points and a consistent method, not only a visual diagram.</li>
    <li><strong>Target measurements and tolerances:</strong> record expected finished measurements and what variation is acceptable.</li>
    <li><strong>Fabric and stretch notes:</strong> identify the approved fabric direction and any measurement method needed for stretch-sensitive parts.</li>
    <li><strong>Fit comments:</strong> document decisions about waist hold, support, coverage, length and ease so they are not lost between sample rounds.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="review">Review the grade before bulk production</h2>
  <p>When a project includes several sizes, do not approve only the base size. Ask how the key points will change across the range and review at least the sizes most likely to expose a problem. For example, a high waistband may become too tall or too tight; a strap may not lengthen enough; a leg opening or armhole may shift the intended silhouette.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Confirm the approved base sample and its full measurement record.</li>
    <li>Review the proposed size chart and grade rules for the product category.</li>
    <li>Check at least representative graded sizes where the product has high stretch, support or coverage sensitivity.</li>
    <li>Record sample comments by measurement point, not only general feedback such as “fit better.”</li>
    <li>Lock the approved specification, logo placement, label details and packaging instructions before bulk cutting.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>FITCLO serves global activewear buyers with ready-stock, private-label and OEM/ODM routes. For a stock style, confirm the available size range and sample fit before ordering. For custom development, use the measurement specification and sample feedback to keep the final graded production aligned with the approved product direction.</p>

